\description{
Fits normal mixture models (NMM) to morphological trait blocks derived from
a morphometric matrix, and generates a standard set of diagnostic figures
and tables for each trait block analyzed. The function is designed to work
directly on the objects produced by \code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}}.

For each requested trait block, the function fits a PCA to rank traits by
their contribution to the first principal component, retains the top
contributing traits, fits a Gaussian mixture model with
\code{\link[mclust:Mclust]{mclust::Mclust()}} — which selects both the covariance structure and the
number of components by BIC — and saves a BIC plot, a classification plot,
an uncertainty plot, and a spreadsheet of group assignments.
}
\details{
\code{morph_nmm()} expects \code{analysis_data} as produced by
\code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}}: a numeric data frame with \code{id} and \code{taxon}
columns plus one column per morphological trait, with specimen IDs as row
names. The trait blocks (\code{"veg"}, \code{"flo"}, \code{"fru"}, or any subset thereof)
are not passed in separately — they are read directly from the
\code{"base_cols"} attribute that \code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}} attaches to
\code{analysis_data}. If \code{analysis_data} has no \code{"base_cols"} attribute (e.g.
it was not built with \code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}}, or the attribute was
dropped by an intervening subsetting operation), the function stops with an
informative error.

The \code{nmm_blocks} argument controls which trait sets are analyzed:

\itemize{
\item \code{"all"}: runs NMM on each individual block in \code{base_cols}
(typically \code{"veg"}, \code{"flo"}, \code{"fru"}), as well as on every pairwise and
full combination of these blocks (\code{"vegflo"}, \code{"vegfru"}, \code{"flofru"},
\code{"vegflofru"}). Combinations whose source blocks are absent from
\code{base_cols} are silently skipped;
\item a character vector naming one or more blocks in \code{base_cols}: runs
NMM only on the requested block(s), individually.
}

Within each block, rows with missing values are removed via
\code{\link[stats:na.omit]{stats::na.omit()}} and traits with zero variance across the retained
specimens are dropped, since they cannot contribute to a correlation-based
PCA nor to a mixture model. Blocks left with fewer than 2 traits, or with
fewer than \code{nmm_min_n} complete specimens, are skipped with a warning.

Trait selection is based on PCA: a PCA is fit with \code{\link[stats:prcomp]{stats::prcomp()}} using
\code{scale. = TRUE}, the squared loadings on the first principal component are
rescaled to percentages, and the \code{nmm_top_traits} highest-contributing
traits are retained for the mixture model. This step keeps the
dimensionality of the mixture model manageable relative to the number of
specimens; note that it is unsupervised and makes no use of the \code{taxon}
column, so the resulting groups remain independent of the current
taxonomic hypothesis.

The mixture model is fit with \code{\link[mclust:Mclust]{mclust::Mclust()}}. By default the number of
components and the covariance parameterization are both selected by BIC
over the full set of \code{mclust} models; \code{nmm_G} and \code{nmm_model_names}
restrict that search when a particular number of groups or a particular
family of models is required. Model fitting is wrapped in \code{\link[=tryCatch]{tryCatch()}}, so
a block whose model fails to converge produces a warning and is skipped
rather than aborting the run. \code{nmm_seed} is applied before each block for
reproducibility.

For each analyzed block, the following figures are generated:

\itemize{
\item a BIC plot across covariance models and numbers of components
(\code{\link[factoextra:fviz_mclust_bic]{factoextra::fviz_mclust_bic()}});
\item a classification plot showing specimens projected onto the first
two principal components of the retained traits, coloured by mixture
component;
\item an uncertainty plot, in which point size reflects the uncertainty
of each specimen's assignment.
